Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
